-- APPLY-ONE — c0 §43 `law.brace`, §44 `law.apply.one` / `law.construct.zero` /
-- `law.pack.shape`. gap[088] is the defect; gap[092] carries what is still owed.
--
-- THE LAW. `name{ … }` is ONE form and it is APPLICATION; the SUBJECT decides
-- what applying means. A descriptor subject constructs, a callable subject
-- applies, and neither is a diagnostic. The parser resolves NOTHING — it emits
-- one structural application node for every face.
--
-- WHAT THIS FIXTURE PINS. This paragraph used to say the descriptor rows were
-- "deliberately not here" because `point{ x = 3.0, y = 4.0 }` still emitted a
-- call to an undeclared `point`. THAT IS STALE: gap[092] closed the resolving
-- half, and a fixture that goes on recording the old state teaches the next
-- reader that the gap is still open. Rows 6-7 below are the descriptor subject,
-- by value; the rows needing a descriptor-TYPED callee are in
-- `examples/apply_one_descriptor_proof.id` and ROW 8 says why.
--
-- Every row asserts a VALUE. `tostring(d)` once reported `d=table` for a
-- decoder returning an EMPTY table and a bisect built on that was wrong, so
-- "it compiled" and "it built something" are not evidence here.
--
-- SEPARATE DEFECT, measured while writing this and NOT caused by APPLY-ONE: a
-- module-scope typed record binding beside a `main()` emits `duo_g__p` against
-- a `duo_g_p` declaration and the C compiler refuses it. Reproduces with no
-- brace face anywhere in the file. That is why `p` lives inside `main` here.

point: @{ x: f64, y: f64 }

-- The CALLABLE subjects. `apply(callable)(argpack)` is one of the four edges
-- §44 `apply.edge` enumerates, and these rows prove the brace face reaches its
-- subject and answers. Read against rows 6-7: the SAME face, a different
-- subject, and the subject is what decides which edge is taken.
first(t: any): any
    t.x

second(t: any): any
    t.y

kind = @{ eof = 0, ident = 1 }

main(): void
    -- ROW 1 — the anonymous pack, applied to nothing. c0 §44a: "`{ x = 1,
    -- y = 2 }` an ordinary anonymous structured value / `point{ x = 1, y = 2 }`
    -- APPLICATION of `point` to that shape — different because one has a
    -- SUBJECT." This row is the one WITHOUT a subject, and it is the
    -- construction path that has always worked: a typed binding plus a bare
    -- pack, two forms already in the grammar, composed.
    p: point = { x = 3.0, y = 4.0 }
    print(p.x)
    print(p.y)

    -- ROW 2 — the CALLABLE subject through the BRACE face, and ROW 3 the same
    -- subject through the PAREN face. §44: "parenthesized, braced and string
    -- faces project onto the SAME application relation", so these two must
    -- agree. If they ever disagree the faces have stopped being faces, and this
    -- pair is what convicts that.
    braced = first{ x = 7.0, y = 8.0 }
    parened = first({ x = 7.0, y = 8.0 })
    print(braced)
    print(parened)

    -- ROW 4 — a second label read off the same pack. §44 `apply.carries` names
    -- "argument labels" and "argument positions"; both are the pack's own
    -- fields in declaration order, which is why APPLY-ONE added nothing
    -- parallel to hold them — a second table of labels is where the two shapes
    -- drift apart.
    print(second{ x = 1.0, y = 9.0 })

    -- ROW 5 — `@{ … }`. c0 §43 `anchor.brace` recovers the elided name from the
    -- ENCLOSING DESCRIPTOR; at top level there is none, so the pack is honestly
    -- anonymous and `@` is doing its OTHER job, staging. Not a third mechanism:
    -- one reader, a different stance.
    print(kind.ident)

    -- ROW 6 — THE DESCRIPTOR SUBJECT. c0 §43 `law.brace` row 1, §44
    -- `apply.edge`: "apply(descriptor)(fieldpack) -> a value the descriptor
    -- describes". This is the row gap[092] owed, and the reason the gap existed
    -- is worth keeping next to it: the face reached its subject all along, and
    -- nothing downstream asked what the subject WAS, so the call path won
    -- unconditionally and this read `call to undeclared function 'point'`.
    --
    -- Read against ROW 1. Row 1 constructs a `point` through the two forms that
    -- always worked — a typed binding plus a bare pack — and this row builds the
    -- SAME value through the applied face with no annotation to lean on. Same
    -- digits from two edges, or one of them has stopped describing `point`.
    q = point{ x = 3.0, y = 4.0 }
    print(q.x)
    print(q.y)

    -- ROW 7 — POSITIONS as well as labels. §44 `apply.carries` names both, and
    -- both are the pack's OWN fields in declaration order. Nothing parallel
    -- holds them, which is why this needed no machinery of its own — a second
    -- table of labels beside the pack is where the two shapes drift apart.
    r = point{ 5.0, 6.0 }
    print(r.x)

    -- ROW 8 — COMPOSITION and REPRESENTATION are in
    -- `examples/apply_one_descriptor_proof.id`, not here, for a MEASURED
    -- reason rather than a preference. A top-level function with a non-`any`
    -- return type, in the same file as `kind`, makes ROW 5's `print(kind.ident)`
    -- emit `lua_to_display_str(1)` — an `int` where a `lua_Value` is wanted,
    -- which the C compiler refuses. Confirmed on the pre-gap[092] compiler
    -- (`HEAD~1`, built in isolation and run against the same nine lines), so it
    -- is not this work. Same class as the `duo_g__p` mangling noted above, and
    -- it is recorded rather than stepped around silently: a fixture that quietly
    -- avoids a defect hides it.
end
